Celiac disease
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Chronic steatorrhoea, Refractory iron deficiency anaemia, Dermatitis herpetiformis, Positive tTG IgA with villous atrophy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-007
Reason for Visit: Severe, dehydrating diarrhoea over the past 48 hours superimposed on chronic profound lethargy and recent fainting episode.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient, roommate.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified an acute exacerbation of chronic gastrointestinal symptoms (high-volume diarrhoea) in a patient with a history of profound lethargy and a recent syncopal episode. This constellation raised immediate concern for severe electrolyte derangement and severe anaemia requiring urgent physical evaluation and fluid resuscitation to prevent cardiovascular collapse prior to routine clinic hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I'm passing massive, foul-smelling liquid stools constantly, and I feel so weak I passed out when I stood up."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a 6-month history of chronic episodic diarrhoea, describing bulky, foul-smelling, floating stools indicating severe steatorrhoea. Associated with recurrent abdominal bloating, significantly increased flatulence, and non-specific persistent crampy abdominal discomfort. Notes generalized fatigue, profound lethargy, and an unintentional 5kg weight loss despite adequate caloric intake. Recently diagnosed by GP with unexplained iron deficiency anaemia completely refractory to oral iron supplementation. Reports intensely pruritic, grouped blisters on elbows and knees.
Functional Status: Currently resting in bed, orthostatic symptoms upon standing.
Social/Safety Concerns: Shared living arrangement, roommate providing fluids but patient struggling to maintain intake.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 95/60 (Supine), 80/50 (Standing) | HR: 110 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 58 Kg
General: Patient appears pale, cachectic, and fatigued. Dry mucous membranes.
Physical Exam: Abdomen is distended, tympanic to percussion, with hyperactive bowel sounds; mild generalized tenderness without peritonism. Skin exam reveals excoriated, grouped vesicles on the extensor surfaces of the elbows bilaterally (consistent with dermatitis herpetiformis). Positive orthostatic vitals noted. Clinical picture strongly suggests chronic steatorrhoea, refractory iron deficiency anaemia, and dermatitis herpetiformis; positive tTG IgA with villous atrophy is presumed pending serology/endoscopy.
Environmental Observations: Multiple over-the-counter anti-diarrhoeal and iron supplement bottles visible on bedside table.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking oral ferrous sulfate with zero improvement in fatigue.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Celiac disease - Status: Exacerbated (Acute diarrhoeal illness on background of severe malabsorption)
Differentials:

Crohn's Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of classic dermatitis herpetiformis and primarily malabsorptive symptoms (steatorrhoea, refractory iron deficiency) without frank hematochezia, severe localized right lower quadrant pain, or perianal disease.

Pancreatic Exocrine Insufficiency - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Concomitant presence of the intensely pruritic vesicular skin rash (dermatitis herpetiformis) and absence of chronic severe epigastric pain radiating to the back.

Infectious Gastroenteritis (e.g., Giardia) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The prolonged, 6-month chronic timeline with systemic autoimmune manifestations (refractory anaemia, characteristic skin rash).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Risk of falls due to orthostasis)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute-on-chronic volume depletion secondary to high-volume steatorrhoea, compounded by profound underlying malabsorptive anaemia. The objective finding of significant orthostatic hypotension and clinical signs of dehydration in a patient unable to maintain oral intake demanded an urgent, face-to-face after-hours assessment. Deferring care until normal operating hours posed an unacceptable risk of severe hypovolaemic shock or injury from a syncopal fall. The immediate provision of oral rehydration protocols and anti-motility agents was necessary to stabilize the patient overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Loperamide 4mg immediately. Initiated strict oral rehydration therapy (ORT) with prescribed electrolyte sachets. Advised urgent fasting blood tests (tTG-IgA, total IgA, FBC, Iron studies, CMP) first thing Monday morning.
Positive Behavioral Change: Mix one full sachet of oral rehydration salts with exactly 250ml of water and take small sips continuously over an hour. This precise concentration actively exploits the sodium-glucose co-transporter in your gut, forcing your body to absorb water even when the bowel is inflamed, thereby reversing your severe dehydration and dizziness.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: GP review within 48 hours to review bloods and facilitate urgent Gastroenterology referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If you remain dizzy while lying down, pass out again, stop producing urine for more than 8 hours, or develop a fever >38.0°C, call an ambulance immediately.

